BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:coding-it-forward-winter-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261020T144136Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261025
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261026
SUMMARY:Deadline: Coding it Forward: Winter Fellowship 2027 (US civic tech 
 placements)
DESCRIPTION:Coding it Forward's Winter Fellowship is a paid\, full-time\, t
 en-week job placement that embeds early-career technologists in city\, sta
 te and federal government offices across the United States. The Winter 202
 7 cohort (11 January to 19 March 2027) hires for roles in data\, design\, 
 product and software engineering in partnership with the State of North Ca
 rolina and the State of New Jersey\; some roles are fully remote and some 
 involve an in-person component. Fellows receive a mentor for professional 
 development\, weekly public interest technology educational events\, cohor
 t check-in calls\, community events and access to a 700+ alumni network. P
 AY: USD 20 per hour with a Bachelor's degree\, USD 25 per hour pursuing or
  holding a graduate degree\; pay and dates cannot be negotiated. ELIGIBILI
 TY: strictly recent graduates whose most recent graduation date falls betw
 een 30 April 2025 and 1 January 2027\, not actively enrolled in school dur
 ing the fellowship\, at least 18 years old by 11 January 2027\, available 
 for a full-time commitment with no additional employment\; must be a US ci
 tizen\, US national or permanent resident physically residing in the US fo
 r the duration (international students are ineligible regardless of visa t
 ype or CPT/OPT authorization). APPLICATION: pick one track (data\, design\
 , product or software engineering) and submit short answers plus a standar
 dized resume (and a design portfolio where applicable) via the Coding it F
 orward application portal\; role descriptions are published on the program
 's public Notion page. Interviews with Coding it Forward and with governme
 nt partners run mid to late November 2026\, offers in early December 2026.
  DEADLINE: 25 October 2026\, 11:59pm PT\, reviewed on a rolling basis.\n\n
 Organisation: Coding it Forward\nLocation: United States. Winter 2027 plac
 ements are with the State of North Carolina and the State of New Jersey\; 
 some roles are fully remote (within the US)\, some involve an in-person co
 mponent. Applicants must be US citizens\, US nationals or permanent reside
 nts and must physically reside in the US for the duration.\nAward: Paid fu
 ll-time job placement: USD 20 per hour for applicants with a Bachelor's de
 gree\, USD 25 per hour for applicants pursuing or holding a graduate degre
 e (Master's or PhD)\, at 40 hours per week for ten weeks (roughly USD 8\,0
 00 to 10\,000 in total). Pay rates and work dates are non-negotiable. No a
 pplication fee.\nDuration: Ten weeks full-time (40 hours per week)\, 11 Ja
 nuary to 19 March 2027.\n\nMore: https://codingitforward.com/fellowship?ut
 m_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://codingitforward.com/fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugran
 tdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:United States. Winter 2027 placements are with the State of North 
 Carolina and the State of New Jersey\; some roles are fully remote (within
  the US)\, some involve an in-person component. Applicants must be US citi
 zens\, US nationals or permanent residents and must physically reside in t
 he US for the duration.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:iaps-ai-policy-fellowship-spring-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261020T144136Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260927
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260928
SUMMARY:Deadline: IAPS: AI Policy Fellowship Spring 2027 (DC\, London or re
 mote)
DESCRIPTION:The Institute for AI Policy and Strategy (IAPS) runs a three-mo
 nth\, fully funded AI policy fellowship placing fellows on concrete AI gov
 ernance and policy research projects. Spring 2027 cohort: 22 February to 1
 4 May 2027\, opening with a mandatory in-person component in Washington DC
  from 22 February to 5 March 2027\, after which fellows work from the DC o
 ffice\, the London office or remotely. STIPENDS: USD 18\,000 for Fellows a
 nd USD 24\,000 for Senior Fellows\, plus USD 3\,000 extra on the in-person
  DC or London tracks\, healthcare benefits and a USD 500 professional deve
 lopment stipend. ELIGIBILITY: no technical background or prior policy expe
 rience required\; the DC track requires the right to work in the US betwee
 n 22 February and 14 May 2027\, and no visa sponsorship is available for t
 he US or UK\; the remote track is open globally with country-specific exce
 ptions. TIMELINE: initial responses mid-October 2026\, live interviews in 
 November\, final selections early December. Deadline 27 September 2026. Ap
 ply at https://www.guidedtrack.com/programs/ll4vpg7/run.\n\nOrganisation: 
 Institute for AI Policy and Strategy (IAPS)\nLocation: Washington DC or Lo
 ndon track (desk space provided)\, or fully remote from most countries. Ma
 ndatory in-person opening in Washington DC\, 22 February to 5 March 2027. 
 No visa sponsorship for the US or UK.\nAward: Fellows receive USD 18\,000 
 and Senior Fellows USD 24\,000 as a stipend for the three-month full-time 
 program\, plus an additional USD 3\,000 stipend on the in-person DC or Lon
 don tracks\, healthcare benefits and a USD 500 professional development st
 ipend. Up to 45 fellows. No application fee.\nDuration: Three months full-
 time\, 22 February to 14 May 2027\; extensions of 4 to 12 weeks were offer
 ed to more than half of the previous cohort\n\nMore: https://www.iaps.ai/f
 ellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.iaps.ai/fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&ut
 m_medium=referral
LOCATION:Washington DC or London track (desk space provided)\, or fully rem
 ote from most countries. Mandatory in-person opening in Washington DC\, 22
  February to 5 March 2027. No visa sponsorship for the US or UK.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:collider-program-manager-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261020T144136Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260823
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260824
SUMMARY:Deadline: Collider: Program Manager (AI safety fieldbuilding\, New 
 York)
DESCRIPTION:Collider\, the New York coworking and convening space for AI sa
 fety and other high-impact work\, is hiring a Program Manager to design\, 
 run and scale the programmes that grow the AI safety talent pipeline and i
 nfrastructure in New York. The role owns programmes end to end\, from conc
 ept to launch to iteration\, working with researchers\, practitioners\, ed
 ucators\, funders and partner organisations. WHAT YOU DO: design and opera
 te fieldbuilding programmes (fellowships\, training programmes\, workshops
 \, conferences\, cohort courses and new formats)\; own budget\, timeline\,
  vendor relationships\, participant experience and post-programme evaluati
 on\; recruit and coordinate mentors\, instructors\, advisors and participa
 nts\, and build the pipelines and selection processes that hold quality as
  things scale\; define and track outcomes with metrics beyond participant 
 satisfaction\, and iterate on the evidence\; maintain relationships across
  research labs\, university groups\, training programmes (MATS\, ARENA\, B
 lueDot\, SPAR\, AI Safety Camp are named)\, grantmakers and adjacent commu
 nities\; represent Collider externally\; write programme reports\, strateg
 y memos\, post-mortems and public materials\; and work with leadership on 
 which programmes to launch\, scale or sunset. REQUIRED: 3+ years of progra
 mme or project management\, ideally running cohort\, fellowship or trainin
 g programmes\; substantive understanding of the AI safety field\, its orga
 nisations\, its main research agendas (alignment\, interpretability\, eval
 uations\, governance) and its open problems\; strong operational instincts
 \; an entrepreneurial\, initiative-taking approach to judgement calls abou
 t people\, partnerships and priorities\; and clear written communication. 
 NICE TO HAVE: prior experience at an AI lab\, AI safety organisation\, uni
 versity group\, training programme or grantmaker\; a background in educati
 on\, talent development or community building\; enough ML or alignment lit
 eracy to follow a paper's argument\; policy literacy on the AI governance 
 landscape\; or a background in finance\, law or another professional field
  Collider wants to build pipelines into. PROCESS: apply via the form in th
 e job document by 23 August 2026 (applications are reviewed on a rolling b
 asis and an offer may be made before the deadline\, so applying early is a
 dvised)\, then a 15 to 25 minute screening call with hiring partner Impact
  Ops\, an interview with the prospective manager\, a paid one-day in-perso
 n work trial at the Collider coworking space\, and 2 to 3 references. Repo
 rts to the Director.\n\nOrganisation: Collider\nLocation: Hybrid in New Yo
 rk City\, USA. No visa sponsorship: candidates must already have the right
  to work in the US.\nAward: USD 150\,000 to 180\,000 base salary\, plus he
 alth insurance\, 401(k) and 20 days paid time off. The one-day in-person w
 ork trial is paid.\nDuration: Full-time\, permanent\; start date flexible\
 n\nMore: https://docs.google.com/document/d/1cP3r4slpewE6_5wsJQYTPAg_qKsRX
 4WYDSy7bfYxMd0/preview?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=re
 ferral
URL:https://docs.google.com/document/d/1cP3r4slpewE6_5wsJQYTPAg_qKsRX4WYDSy
 7bfYxMd0/preview?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Hybrid in New York City\, USA. No visa sponsorship: candidates mus
 t already have the right to work in the US.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:american-academy-berlin-prize-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261020T144136Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260925
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260926
SUMMARY:Deadline: American Academy in Berlin: Berlin Prize Fellowship 2027-
 28
DESCRIPTION:The American Academy in Berlin is an independent\, nonprofit in
 stitute for advanced study in the arts\, humanities\, social sciences\, la
 w\, journalism and public policy\, whose primary mission is to strengthen 
 the cultural and intellectual ties between the United States and Germany. 
 Each year\, after a rigorous selection process\, the Academy awards roughl
 y two dozen semester-long Berlin Prize fellowships to individuals who demo
 nstrate excellence in their fields. DISCIPLINES: applications are welcome 
 across the humanities\, social sciences\, law\, journalism and various are
 as of public policy. The Academy generally encourages projects addressing 
 pressing issues of the day\, specifically naming democracy\, security\, te
 chnology and society (especially AI)\, energy including climate\, and glob
 al order. Dedicated fellowships also support projects in Jewish studies\, 
 German studies\, and public health and biotechnology. Project proposals ne
 ed not focus on Germany\, but candidates should explain how a residency in
  Berlin would enhance their work. NOT ACCEPTED: proposals in mathematics o
 r the natural sciences. NOTE FOR ARTISTS: visual artists and composers are
  invited through separate\, nomination-based competitions\, so there is no
  open application route for them here. ELIGIBILITY: fellowships are restri
 cted to citizens and permanent residents of the United States. Both emergi
 ng and established figures are eligible. PhD holders qualify as emerging s
 cholars with a strong record of peer-reviewed work beyond the dissertation
 \; candidates in non-academic fields such as journalism\, filmmaking and p
 olicy need a significant record of publication or production\; fiction and
  nonfiction writers must have published at least one book with a reputable
  press. WHAT FELLOWS DO: fellows present their work to audiences in and ar
 ound Berlin through lectures\, readings\, public discussions\, performance
 s and film screenings\, and should be willing to engage with relevant Germ
 an institutions\, organizations and the media\; these activities are integ
 ral to the Academy's mission of fostering transatlantic dialogue. APPLICAT
 ION MATERIALS: a project proposal of 1\,500 to 1\,750 words plus a one-pag
 e bibliography\; a curriculum vitae of up to 10 pages\; two writing sample
 s totalling no more than 60 pages\; and three letters of recommendation\, 
 which are due 2 October 2026. DEADLINE: Friday 25 September 2026 at 12:00 
 noon Eastern Time (18:00 Central European Time). TIMELINE: all candidates 
 are notified of the competition outcome by email in late March 2027\; the 
 2027-28 Berlin Prize recipients are announced in May 2027.\n\nOrganisation
 : American Academy in Berlin\nLocation: Berlin\, Germany\, at the Hans Arn
 hold Center. RESTRICTED: fellowships are open only to citizens and permane
 nt residents of the United States.\nAward: Monthly stipend of USD 5\,000\,
  plus roundtrip airfare\, accommodation at or near the Hans Arnhold Center
 \, partial board\, in-house library services and professional networking o
 pportunities. Roughly two dozen fellowships awarded each year. No applicat
 ion fee.\nDuration: One full academic semester (about 4 months). Fall runs
  mid-August to mid-December\; spring runs late January to late May.\n\nMor
 e: https://www.americanacademy.de/apply/apply-for-a-fellowship/?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.americanacademy.de/apply/apply-for-a-fellowship/?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Berlin\, Germany\, at the Hans Arnhold Center. RESTRICTED: fellows
 hips are open only to citizens and permanent residents of the United State
 s.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:zwillgen-fellowship-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261020T144136Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260831
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260901
SUMMARY:Deadline: ZwillGen Fellowship Program 2027-2028 (Technology Law)
DESCRIPTION:The ZwillGen Fellowship is a 12-month position for recent law-s
 chool graduates and early-career attorneys interested in working at the in
 tersection of law\, technology\, and public policy. Fellows work alongside
  attorneys on matters involving privacy\, cybersecurity\, artificial intel
 ligence\, online platforms\, surveillance\, digital advertising\, fintech\
 , online safety\, and technology regulation\, contributing to client couns
 eling\, investigations\, litigation\, and emerging regulatory matters\, as
  well as research\, writing\, and speaking. The program includes a reduced
  billable target (1\,250 hours) to make room for thought leadership and en
 gagement with the technology policy\, privacy\, and civil liberties commun
 ity. ELIGIBILITY: must have graduated from law school before the program s
 tarts\; must have taken or plan to take a bar exam near the start of the F
 ellowship\; must be able to work out of the DC office and be eligible to a
 pply for admission to the DC Bar. Candidates primarily interested in tradi
 tional IP practice may find it less aligned. A small number of applicants 
 may also be considered for the Hannah Schaller Memorial Skylark Fellowship
  (privacy-law focus\, with a reduced 1\,100-hour billable target for addit
 ional community engagement). Following the Fellowship\, fellows may be inv
 ited into a full-time attorney role or supported in pursuing other technol
 ogy law and policy opportunities. APPLY with a resume\, law-school transcr
 ipt\, writing sample\, and cover letter by 31 August 2026.\n\nOrganisation
 : ZwillGen\nLocation: Washington\, D.C.\, USA (must work out of the DC off
 ice)\nAward: Salary USD 115\,000 to USD 125\,000\, with a reduced annual b
 illable target (1\,250 hours) to create space for writing\, professional d
 evelopment\, and policy engagement. No application fee.\nDuration: 12-mont
 h position beginning Fall 2027\n\nMore: https://www.zwillgen.com/careers/f
 ellowship-program/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referr
 al
URL:https://www.zwillgen.com/careers/fellowship-program/?utm_source=artific
 ialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Washington\, D.C.\, USA (must work out of the DC office)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:alicia-patterson-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261020T144136Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261001
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261002
SUMMARY:Deadline: Alicia Patterson Fellowship 2027 (Fund for Investigative 
 Journalism)
DESCRIPTION:Long-running fellowship for in-depth\, investigative written re
 porting on subjects of public interest\, now administered by the Fund for 
 Investigative Journalism after the foundation's December 2025 merger. For 
 seasoned reporters with a body of in-depth written work\; freelancers are 
 encouraged and staff journalists may apply if they take leave (part-time w
 ork is allowed during the fellowship\, full-time is not). Twelve-month fel
 lows produce four articles\, six-month fellows two. Decisions by end of 20
 26. Distinct from FIJ's regular reporting grants. No application fee.\n\nO
 rganisation: Fund for Investigative Journalism (Alicia Patterson Foundatio
 n program)\nLocation: Remote\; fellows may live anywhere\, but projects mu
 st be U.S.-focused for publication in American outlets\nAward: $40\,000 (1
 2-month fellowship) or $20\,000 (6-month fellowship)\, paid monthly\; cove
 rs living and reporting costs\nDuration: 6 or 12 months\, beginning early 
 2027\n\nMore: https://investigate.submittable.com/submit/355041/alicia-pat
 terson-fellowship-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=re
 ferral
URL:https://investigate.submittable.com/submit/355041/alicia-patterson-fell
 owship-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote\; fellows may live anywhere\, but projects must be U.S.-foc
 used for publication in American outlets
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:horizon-fellowship-2027-cohort@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261020T144136Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260830
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260831
SUMMARY:Deadline: Horizon Fellowship\, 2027 Cohort
DESCRIPTION:The Horizon Fellowship places technologists\, scientists and re
 searchers with emerging-technology expertise into US federal agencies\, co
 ngressional offices and Washington DC think tanks to work on AI\, biotechn
 ology and emerging-technology policy. The 2027 cohort runs three tiers: ju
 nior fellows (under three years of experience\, no PhD) at a USD 78\,000 b
 ase for six months\; fellows (three or more years of relevant experience\,
  or a PhD) at USD 130\,000 for twelve months\; and a newly added senior fe
 llow tier (twelve or more years of experience with a leadership background
 ) at USD 190\,000 or more for twelve months. All tiers receive a USD 17\,0
 00 benefits stipend\, a USD 3\,000 professional development stipend and up
  to USD 3\,000 in relocation assistance\, and all are potentially renewabl
 e. The programme year begins with part-time remote training from January t
 o March 2027\, followed by matching in April and May and placement from sp
 ring or summer 2027 onward. Most offers are expected by the end of Novembe
 r 2026. Placements run up to two years in total: fellows and senior fellow
 s take a one-year term at a federal agency\, congressional office or think
  tank with the option to renew for a second one-year term\, potentially on
  a different institutional track\, while junior think tank fellows take a 
 six-month term renewable once. Horizon reports that nearly all finalists a
 cross its five cohorts to date have secured a placement at a host organiza
 tion. WHAT THE FELLOWSHIP INCLUDES: dedicated training on the policy lands
 cape and applied skills such as writing and policy analysis\; a tight-knit
  peer community\; introductions to host offices central to US emerging tec
 hnology policy\; and mentorship from senior leaders and the broader Horizo
 n community. ELIGIBILITY: no prior policy experience or specific degree is
  required\, and Horizon explicitly welcomes candidates from diverse backgr
 ounds\; fellows must reside in the Washington DC area for the placement. W
 ORK AUTHORISATION: applicants need pre-existing work authorisation that do
 es not depend on employer sponsorship. Non-US citizens on E-3\, TN or H-1B
 1 visas may qualify case by case\; F-1 OPT during the first year after gra
 duation is acceptable but STEM OPT extensions are not\; green card holders
  are eligible but may face restrictions on executive branch roles. Junior 
 fellows have no specific degree requirement and may apply while finishing 
 college\, but are limited to the think tank track. SELECTION PROCESS (Sept
 ember to November 2026): application decision\, then a paid work sample of
  up to 6 hours\, a 20-minute screening interview\, a 40-minute technical i
 nterview and a 25-minute final interview. Applications close 30 August 202
 6 at 11:59pm Anywhere on Earth.\n\nOrganisation: Horizon Institute for Pub
 lic Service\nLocation: Washington DC\, USA (residence required for placeme
 nt). Part-time remote training January to March 2027.\nAward: Base salary 
 of USD 78\,000 for junior fellows\, USD 130\,000 for fellows\, and USD 190
 \,000 or more for senior fellows\, plus a USD 17\,000 benefits stipend\, a
  USD 3\,000 professional development stipend\, and up to USD 3\,000 reloca
 tion assistance. No application fee.\nDuration: 6 months for junior fellow
 s\, 12 months for fellows and senior fellows\, with potential renewal. Par
 t-time remote training January to March 2027\, matching April to May 2027\
 , placement from summer 2027.\n\nMore: https://horizonpublicservice.org/pr
 ograms/become-a-fellow/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=r
 eferral
URL:https://horizonpublicservice.org/programs/become-a-fellow/?utm_source=a
 rtificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Washington DC\, USA (residence required for placement). Part-time 
 remote training January to March 2027.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:aaas-stpf-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261020T144136Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261101
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261102
SUMMARY:Deadline: Science & Technology Policy Fellowships\, 2027-28 Class
DESCRIPTION:The AAAS Science & Technology Policy Fellowships place scientis
 ts and engineers inside US federal agencies\, congressional offices and th
 e judicial branch for a year of hands-on policymaking\, contributing techn
 ical knowledge and analytical skills to the federal policy process. AI\, d
 ata and computing policy placements are among the largest tracks\, and AAA
 S runs a dedicated AI legislative branch cohort that places AI experts in 
 congressional offices at GS-12 Step 10 on the DC locality scale. ELIGIBILI
 TY: US citizens holding a PhD or equivalent doctoral degree in any science
  or engineering field\, or a master's degree in engineering plus three yea
 rs of post-degree professional experience\; some tracks accept a bachelor'
 s plus five years. Relevant expertise includes AI\, machine learning\, com
 puter science\, robotics and data science. Fellows must reside in DC\, Mar
 yland or northern Virginia during the fellowship year. TIMELINE: the appli
 cation portal opens 1 August 2026 and closes 1 November 2026 at 11:59pm ET
 \, with eligibility notification in late November\, interview notification
  January to February 2027\, semi-finalist interviews in March\, placement 
 offers by early May\, and the fellowship year running 1 September 2027 to 
 31 August 2028. Apply through fellowshipapp.aaas.org.\n\nOrganisation: Ame
 rican Association for the Advancement of Science (AAAS)\nLocation: Washing
 ton DC\, USA. Fellows are placed in US federal executive\, legislative and
  judicial branch offices and must reside in the DC area.\nAward: Stipend d
 epending on experience and placement\, roughly USD 85\,000 to 140\,000\; t
 he dedicated AI legislative branch cohort is paid at GS-12 Step 10 on the 
 DC locality scale. Includes health insurance\, a travel and professional d
 evelopment allowance\, and relocation support for some placements. No appl
 ication fee.\nDuration: One year\, 1 September 2027 to 31 August 2028\, re
 newable in some placements\n\nMore: https://www.aaas.org/programs/science-
 technology-policy-fellowships?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_me
 dium=referral
URL:https://www.aaas.org/programs/science-technology-policy-fellowships?utm
 _source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Washington DC\, USA. Fellows are placed in US federal executive\, 
 legislative and judicial branch offices and must reside in the DC area.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:kluge-fellowships-library-of-congress-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261020T144136Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260915
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260916
SUMMARY:Deadline: Kluge Fellowships at the Library of Congress 2027
DESCRIPTION:The John W. Kluge Center awards twelve residential fellowships 
 a year for humanistic and social science research that draws on the Librar
 y of Congress collections\, with interdisciplinary and cross-cultural proj
 ects particularly welcome. Fellows get four to eleven months in residence 
 at a stipend of USD 5\,000 per month\, work and discussion space in the Je
 fferson Building\, and access to the Library's specialist staff\, the worl
 d's largest law library\, extensive multilingual books and periodicals\, a
 nd deep special collections of manuscripts\, maps\, music\, films\, record
 ed sound\, prints and photographs\, alongside the wider scholarly communit
 y in Washington. The Center was established in 2000 through a USD 60 milli
 on endowment from John W. Kluge. ELIGIBILITY: scholars who received a term
 inal advanced degree (the highest degree available in the discipline\, usu
 ally a PhD\, JD or international equivalent) within the past seven years\,
  in the humanities\, social sciences\, or a professional field such as arc
 hitecture or law. The degree must be in hand at the time of submission\, a
 nd full-time students are therefore out of scope. Open to U.S. citizens an
 d foreign nationals\; selected foreign nationals are assisted in obtaining
  a Library-sponsored J-1 visa\, though holders of H-1B visas are not eligi
 ble. APPLICATION: through the Library's online portal\, opening 15 April 2
 026 and closing 11:59pm on 15 September 2026. Materials required are an ap
 plication form in English\, a CV (max 2 pages)\, a 500-word research abstr
 act\, a one-page statement on how the Library's collections will serve the
  work\, a project proposal with work plan (max 3 pages)\, a bibliography (
 max 3 pages)\, and two reference letters from referees who have read the p
 roposal. No fee.\n\nOrganisation: John W. Kluge Center\, Library of Congre
 ss\nLocation: Washington\, DC\, United States (in residence at the Thomas 
 Jefferson Building\, 101 Independence Ave SE). Open to applicants of any n
 ationality: U.S. citizens and foreign nationals alike\, with J-1 exchange 
 visitor visa sponsorship provided for those who need it.\nAward: Stipend o
 f USD 5\,000 per month for a residency of four to eleven months\, so USD 2
 0\,000 to USD 55\,000 depending on the tenure awarded. Twelve Kluge Fellow
 ships are awarded each year. Stipends are paid monthly by electronic trans
 fer to a U.S. bank account. No application fee\, no participation fee.\nDu
 ration: Four to eleven months in residence\; fellows may take up residence
  at any point in the 18-month window after receiving the fellowship letter
 \n\nMore: https://www.loc.gov/programs/john-w-kluge-center/chairs-fellowsh
 ips/fellowships/kluge-fellowships/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&u
 tm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.loc.gov/programs/john-w-kluge-center/chairs-fellowships/fel
 lowships/kluge-fellowships/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medi
 um=referral
LOCATION:Washington\, DC\, United States (in residence at the Thomas Jeffer
 son Building\, 101 Independence Ave SE). Open to applicants of any nationa
 lity: U.S. citizens and foreign nationals alike\, with J-1 exchange visito
 r visa sponsorship provided for those who need it.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:new-public-entrepreneur-in-residence-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261020T144136Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260909
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260910
SUMMARY:Deadline: New_ Public Entrepreneur in Residence\, Pilot Cohort
DESCRIPTION:New_ Public is piloting a virtual Entrepreneur in Residence pro
 gramme for solo founders building alternatives to the mainstream social pl
 atforms. Its framing: people are turning away from algorithmic social medi
 a\, AI bots are taking over the big platforms\, and users are retreating i
 nto private closed spaces\, which leaves an opening for builders working o
 n a new information ecosystem rather than the status quo. The residency su
 pplies research\, experts\, connections and tools\; the founder brings the
  vision and experience. TWO FOCUS AREAS: humans connecting to humans\, mea
 ning products that bring people into meaningful contact online (forums\, m
 essaging apps\, groups\, microblogging\, in-person meetups)\, including AI
 -powered platforms and tools as long as they connect people to people rath
 er than people to bots\; and local and civic information environments\, me
 aning tools for distributing local news\, building community knowledge and
  facilitating collective sensemaking\, so people can get trustworthy infor
 mation about their communities. WHAT RESIDENTS DO: use self-directed time 
 to prototype ideas\, test assumptions and gain clarity\; participate in re
 gular check-ins\, engage with the other resident and the wider team\, and 
 take candid feedback on product and strategy\; engage seriously with go-to
 -market questions about who the user is\, how they find you\, and the path
  to sustainability\; and help shape the programme itself\, since the pilot
  cohort's feedback directly informs how the EIR programme runs going forwa
 rd. WHO THEY WANT: entrepreneurial founders ready to build their next thin
 g within the focus areas\, with a track record of or clear drive toward wo
 rk that builds social trust\, connection and belonging\; strong product th
 inkers with good judgment\, a relentless focus on user needs\, and opennes
 s to coaching on mission-aligned business models. An early idea is enough\
 , no working product required. The pilot is open to founders with somethin
 g else going on alongside\, though the full programme will most likely be 
 full time. APPLICATION PROCESS: submit the written application online\; sh
 ortlisted applicants submit a short video by 18 September 2026 covering ba
 ckground\, motivation and a quick pitch\; full applications and videos go 
 to the advisory board\, with offers made in early October ahead of the kic
 koff. FUTURE COHORTS: New_ Public plans to open the next cohort to the UK 
 and EU from spring 2027. Deadline 11:59pm Pacific on Wednesday 9 September
  2026.\n\nOrganisation: New_ Public\nLocation: Remote\, with an in-person 
 kickoff retreat in Philadelphia\, USA (19 to 22 October 2026). Open to app
 licants based in the United States or Canada.\nAward: Stipend of USD 22\,5
 00\, paid in three monthly installments across the residency\, each contin
 gent on continued active participation. Plus USD 1\,500 toward an AI tool 
 of the resident's choice for coding\, prototyping or design. Two residents
  are selected in the pilot cohort. Also provided: hands-on product and str
 ategy support from senior New_ Public staff and outside experts\; access t
 o New_ Public's five years of original research on moderation\, governance
 \, community stewardship and platform design\, plus direct access to the r
 esearchers behind it\; and introductions across New_ Public's network of m
 ajor platforms\, startups\, university researchers and funders for hiring\
 , fundraising\, early user testing and community partnerships. Residents m
 ust submit a Form W-9 or W-8BEN to receive the stipend. No application fee
 .\nDuration: Three months\, from the week of 19 October 2026 to 29 January
  2027\, run remotely apart from the opening retreat\n\nMore: https://newpu
 blic.org/EIR?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://newpublic.org/EIR?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:Remote\, with an in-person kickoff retreat in Philadelphia\, USA (
 19 to 22 October 2026). Open to applicants based in the United States or C
 anada.
